{"answer": "The critic's counterexample is not established by the target's own provisions and does not succeed against the target as written.\n\n1. The target defines the only route to a status change as a new committed warrant added by a fresh edge-construction pass. It does not state that a pass's internal warrant-generation is unrestricted, cumulative across re-applications, or stateful with respect to prior passes. The counterexample assumes pass-1 can add W1 on first application and W2 on second application to the extended graph. The target supplies no basis for that assumption. The burden is on the critic to establish that the target permits such open-ended, re-application-dependent warrant generation. It is not established.\n\n2. More importantly, the counterexample contradicts the target's stated semantics. The target says the process 'remains deterministic (each pass is a fresh unique fixpoint).' On the critic's own account, pass-1 applied to the graph extended by pass-1 yields a different fixpoint than pass-1 alone. That is exactly a violation of 'each pass is a fresh unique fixpoint.' The critic has not shown that the target authorizes two distinct fixpoints for the same pass; they have assumed it. Under the target's stated uniqueness condition, once pass-1 reaches its fixpoint, re-applying pass-1 to the extended graph must return that same fixpoint. The critic's W2 scenario is therefore inconsistent with the target's explicit constraint, not a consequence of it.\n\n3. The counterexample also assumes monotonic disruption: that adding W2 can flip B from OUT to IN and thereby force A from IN back to UNDEC. But the target's design rationale is that the only effect of a pass is to commit new warrants that can defeat attackers of suspended nodes, enabling previously-suspended nodes to become IN. The critic offers no textual basis for a pass-caused status change in the regressive direction (IN to UNDEC). That direction is the relabeling-style disruption the target explicitly excludes ('never by relabeling an existing graph'). The critic's W2 case is a relabeling of an existing graph's statuses via a side-effect, which the target forbids.\n\n4. The critic's idempotence challenge depends entirely on the unestablished premise that a pass may commit an unbounded, re-application-dependent sequence of distinct warrants. The target says a pass 'may commit new warrants,' but it does not say a pass commits different warrants on each re-application, nor that warrant availability depends on the graph's evolved state in the manner required. 'May commit new warrants' is compatible with a pass computing a single, state-determined fixpoint: the pass inspects the current graph, constructs the warranted edges derivable, commits them, and settles statuses; re-application finds no additional derivable warrants (or, equivalently, reaches the same fixpoint). The target's explicit 'fresh unique fixpoint' language favors this reading. The critic has not excluded it.\n\n5. Therefore the critic has not established that 'pass-1 ◦ pass-1 differs from pass-1' under the target's actual provisions. The counterexample requires (a) re-application-dependent warrant generation, (b) non-unique fixpoints for a single pass, and (c) regressive status changes (IN→UNDEC) caused by a pass — none of which the target states, and the second and third of which the target expressly contradicts. The conjecture of idempotence, read together with the 'fresh unique fixpoint' clause, is internally consistent: each pass computes a unique fixpoint, re-application reproduces it, and composition of a pass with itself returns the same result. The alleged fault is not established; it rests on importing semantics the target does not provide and rejecting semantics the target explicitly states."}